WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:17.137
BEN: Hi, Little Fox readers! On today's People in the News , Olivia will be introducing us to Yo-Yo

2
00:00:17.138 --> 00:00:23.446
Ma, whose musical gifts have made him the most famous cellist in the world. OLIVIA: Hi, everyone. Yo-Yo Ma

3
00:00:23.447 --> 00:00:30.936
was born in1955 in Paris, France. His father was a highly respected music teacher who began teaching Yo-Yo to

4
00:00:30.937 --> 00:00:39.611
play the cello when Yo-Yo was only four. Some of the earliest pieces of music that Yo-Yo learned to

5
00:00:39.612 --> 00:00:50.520
play were Bach's Cello Suites, which are really difficult for most musicians. But Yo-Yo's father had a special technique

6
00:00:50.521 --> 00:00:55.837
for teaching: Yo-Yo learned two measures each day, and by the end of a year, he knew three of

7
00:00:55.838 --> 00:01:04.163
the suites by heart. But Yo-Yo wasn't good merely because he could play difficult pieces of music. He was

8
00:01:04.164 --> 00:01:11.153
able to pour a lot of emotion into his playing, and it wasn't long before other musicians saw him

9
00:01:11.154 --> 00:01:19.725
as a child prodigy. In 1961, when Yo-Yo was six, he and his family moved to New York in the

10
00:01:19.726 --> 00:01:27.822
United States. A year later he and his sister performed in Washington DC for President Kennedy, along with a

11
00:01:27.823 --> 00:01:36.301
crowd of five thousand people. Two years after that, Yo-Yo and his sister performed in Carnegie Hall, one of

12
00:01:36.302 --> 00:01:43.372
the world's most famous concert halls. After completing high school when he was 15, Yo-Yo enrolled in The Juilliard

13
00:01:43.373 --> 00:01:48.631
School, a world-renowned music school. But he soon grew frustrated with spending endless hours in a practice room, and

14
00:01:48.632 --> 00:01:54.715
he began acting out. He rarely listened to his teachers, and he avoided practicing as much as possible. Yo-Yo

15
00:01:54.716 --> 00:02:06.108
eventually realized that he wanted to experience other things in life besides the cello. So he left Juilliard, and

16
00:02:06.109 --> 00:02:11.527
without telling his parents, he enrolled in Harvard University, where he had a chance to learn about subjects such

17
00:02:11.528 --> 00:02:20.003
as history, philosophy, and literature. He became particularly interested in anthropology, the study of other cultures. Yo-Yo's time at

18
00:02:20.004 --> 00:02:25.177
Harvard gave him a much broader view of the world, a view he never would have acquired if he'd

19
00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:32.105
stayed in music school. BEN: What about the cello? Did Yo-Yo still play while he was a student at

20
00:02:32.106 --> 00:02:40.431
Harvard? OLIVIA: Yes, Yo-Yo still dedicated a lot of time to his instrument, performing at student functions and other

21
00:02:40.432 --> 00:02:45.667
events. In addition, he performed professionally around the world, as both a soloist and a member of small groups

22
00:02:45.668 --> 00:02:52.641
and full symphony orchestras. Then, when Yo-Yo was 22, he recorded his first album. Several more followed, and in

23
00:02:52.642 --> 00:02:56.567
1983, he recorded "Bach: Unaccompanied Cello Suites," the same suites his father had taught him when he was first

24
00:02:56.568 --> 00:03:05.290
learning to play. The recording was a huge hit with classical music fans, and it earned Yo-Yo a Grammy—an

25
00:03:05.291 --> 00:03:11.885
important award in the music industry. Yo-Yo went on to record over 75 albums, many of which were critically acclaimed.

26
00:03:11.886 --> 00:03:17.733
He also appeared on several television shows and played cello on some movie soundtracks. In fact, his growing popularity

27
00:03:17.734 --> 00:03:24.057
helped to make classical music a lot more popular in the U.S. Yo-Yo doesn't just play classical music, though.

28
00:03:24.058 --> 00:03:27.061
He has always been interested in other cultures, and that has led him to experiment with the music of

29
00:03:27.062 --> 00:03:33.877
many of those cultures. In 1993 Yo-Yo traveled to the Kalahari Desert in Africa to meet with the Bushmen,

30
00:03:33.878 --> 00:03:45.626
a group of hunter-gatherers he had learned about while studying anthropology in college. Yo-Yo played some classical music for

31
00:03:45.627 --> 00:03:52.276
the Bushmen, and then they played some of their music for him. Yo-Yo learned about their musical traditions and

32
00:03:52.277 --> 00:03:59.013
had an opportunity to play some of their instruments. Yo-Yo has also recorded quite a few nonclassical albums over

33
00:03:59.014 --> 00:04:13.687
the years, collaborating with recording artists from many other genres, including rock, jazz, folk, bluegrass, bossa nova, and tango.

34
00:04:13.688 --> 00:04:17.932
BEN: It sounds like Yo-Yo really enjoys working with other musicians. OLIVIA: He sure does. That's partly because there

35
00:04:17.933 --> 00:04:27.583
isn't a whole lot of music for just the cello, so Yo-Yo doesn't have much choice—he has to work

36
00:04:27.584 --> 00:04:31.581
with other people! But, more importantly, Yo-Yo loves to learn from others. He believes that when two people share

37
00:04:31.582 --> 00:04:38.905
their ideas and talents with each other, both people grow and improve as a result. Yo-Yo's passion for sharing

38
00:04:38.906 --> 00:04:46.563
musical ideas led him to establish the Silk Road Project in 1998. This nonprofit arts organization was named after

39
00:04:46.564 --> 00:04:54.826
the Silk Road, the ancient trade route that connected China to the Roman Empire. Many cultures traded goods and

40
00:04:54.827 --> 00:05:06.284
ideas along this road, and the mission of Yo-Yo's organization echoes this idea, bringing together artists and musicians from

41
00:05:06.285 --> 00:05:17.058
around the world. The Silk Road Project promotes learning and sharing among various cultures and sponsors educational programs in

42
00:05:17.059 --> 00:05:24.788
schools, residencies for musicians, and workshops. The Silk Road Project also spawned the Silk Road Ensemble, a group of musicians

43
00:05:24.789 --> 00:05:30.286
from over twenty different countries. Yo-Yo is the artistic director of the group, as well as a performing member.

44
00:05:30.287 --> 00:05:37.347
The Ensemble tours regularly and plays in all sorts of places, from large concert halls to small art galleries.

45
00:05:37.348 --> 00:05:44.856
Needless to say, Yo-Yo is a very busy man. And maybe that can partly explain another event he is

46
00:05:44.857 --> 00:05:53.444
famous for. One day in 1999 Yo-Yo was so distracted, he actually left his cello behind, in the trunk

47
00:05:53.445 --> 00:06:03.589
of a New York City taxi! The $2.5 million cello was missing for several hours before it was finally

48
00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:07.094
located and returned safely, just in time for his concert that night. BEN: Whoa—good thing Yo-Yo got his cello

49
00:06:07.095 --> 00:06:13.322
back! After learning more about Yo-Yo Ma, there's just one thing I want to do now: listen to some

50
00:06:13.323 --> 00:06:20.521
music. That’s it for this week's People in the News , Little Fox readers. We’ll see you next time!

